engineering.notes:
Read in full: owner-bar.md, brief.md, OWNER-DIRECTION.md, phase1-groups.json (all 28 principles), the P01 members' drafts in phase2.json (J2-06, J7-03, J7-04, J6-16, J8-10, plus J7-06 and J7-07), and phase3.json's clusters, conflicts, recommendation changes and verdict clashes. Also read JOB.md verdicts 3, 8, 23, 43, 44, 52, 53, 61, 77, 89, 93, 94, 101, 103, 104, 107, 108, 113, 114, 150 and 164-166. Spec rules read in the part files: p11 (all of Part XI), TYP-43 and the marker table (p04 572-598), STA-1/STA-3 (p05 470-493), CLO-6/7/10/16/17/18 (p06), ERR-1/3/8/10/11 (p13), FFI-21/22/26/31b/33/33a/33b/33c/27/31 (p16), OBJ-1/3/5, RC-1/4/5/7, WK-11/12/3 (p08), LT-4/LT-45/LT-46 (p07), CELL-1..4 (p09), ALC-1/ALC-2 (p09), EFF-24/25/26 (p10), HR-2a/3/3a/42/42a (p20), and the grammar's bound_list after dyn (p03 72). Nothing was edited or run.

What holds. 164, 165 and 166 are written as lines of the rule and nothing I found is 'really, really better' against them. The creator hand-over (no lock, ordered by shutdown on the home thread then init) is sound at zero cost and really is better than J8-10's drafted A. AnyError's written promise follows rust/anyhow, and its one-thread cost is stated as the critic asked. The (a)/(b) tier split is the right shape.

What does not hold yet, most severe first.
(1) No `thread_shared once fn()`. Consuming workers (`thread.spawn(owns fn() owns job: run(owns job))`) are E3013, and so are consuming scoped tasks. Rust takes FnOnce.
(2) `thread_shared fn()` is ThreadShared by decree, against the rule's own 'nothing else decides them'. CLO-18's closed list of holders leaves `ref` and Copy-view routes open: two threads recv on one Receiver.
(3) The six kinds do not generate the table. THR-9's sentence contradicts the Mutex, guarded by, ref mut and thread_shared fn rows, so std types are decided by name. The general fix is std vouching through P04's one spelling.
(4) Inferable facts the rule refuses:
- `static` is judged unconditionally, while `static mut` and threads=any exports are judged by reach; verdict 8 suffers.
- A scoped task's R must be Send.
- Opening one-thread weakrefs in tasks and @parallel bodies is refused, although WK-12 makes it a read. No verdict covers opening, and a game's parallel AI loop is refused.
(5) Gaps across groups:
- P02: verdict-107 (c) values keep no link, so the rule calls them Send.
- P04: foreign owns T is left undecided, with an interim default that reverses FFI-33b.
- Tier (a) is defined by construct, so borrowed threads(any) C callbacks touch kinds 1-2 unchecked.
(6) RC-4 point 4's 'carries' is ambiguous against verdict 101 fix 2. It is either a cost the design says is zero, or the claimed fix holds for direct owners only.

The low items are wording, or narrow needless refusals and cases the rule leaves undecided:
- Cell reads by two tasks;
- RwLock's Send condition;
- nested scopes reaching static mut;
- `ref t` against 'captured with a word';
- kind 5 in tier (a);
- a lock in tier (a);
- the HR-3 deadlock and its entry fence;
- the error-wrapping words;
- thread_shared dyn I said twice;
- 166's class-family widening, which needs a one-line confirmation.

