design.rule:
Safe code may rely on a fact about foreign code, a value or a type only in two cases. Either the compiler establishes it, from Ember code it reads or from what a C or C++ declaration's own text fixes under that language's rules (types and layouts, `const` on an object, `_Atomic`, `noexcept`, a C++ reference naming one object and never being null). Or someone states it in an `unsafe` item or block that has a `# SAFETY:` note, and `ember tcb` lists the statement. A statement means only what it says, and silence never picks a convenient reading. The compiler follows each fact through every part of a value, at any depth, and along every route a value takes between Ember and foreign code. So a value arriving from C has the type of everything C can send unless a statement narrows it. A safe use that needs an unknown fact is refused when compiling; the message names the fact and gives the line that states it. No statement overrides what the compiler knows to be false. Some callers write no Ember call: a value's end, a virtual or `dyn` call, an operator, a loop, and C calling an export or a callback. They cannot vouch, so everything they reach is safe code. A `drop`, an override or implementation of a safe method, an export and a C callback are never `unsafe fn`; their unsafe steps go in `unsafe:` blocks inside them. Each fact has one way to be stated, and it reaches every kind of item and every type:
- `unsafe extend T implements Send:` and `unsafe extend T implements ThreadShared:`, for any struct, enum or class;
- the same contract words on every slot whose Ember type the compiler maps (hand declarations, overlay entries, exports, callback parameters);
- `@ffi(immutable)` on every foreign static;
- `unsafe import`, exactly when an import's compiled code runs or takes effect with no call.

