I only read; nothing was run. Sources:
- deep/owner-bar.md, brief.md, docs/OWNER-DIRECTION.md;
- phase1-groups.json (all 28 principles, to check for overlap);
- phase2.json entries J8-06/07/08/09/11/14, J5-16, J7-06, J2-12;
- phase3.json clusters K07/08/09/11/15/16/36/46, its conflicts, recommendation changes and verdict clashes;
- JOB.md: goals, keywords, verdicts 1-3, 30-31, 60-62, 91, 101-107, 114, 118-166;
- spec parts: p16-ffi in full, p21-annex-c in full, p09 IX.4 (UNS-1..12), p04 IV.5 and IV.8 (TYP-20/22/24/43, markers), p11 XI.1 (THR-8/9/1/2), p05 (FN-6, CLS-1..11, STA-1/3, ENM-3), p07 (LT-1, DRP-1..8), p06 (CORO-1..6), p15 (iterators), p03 (fn_header grammar), p26 rows for ODR-075/082/111/137/163.

The rule's core holds and is the right shape: facts come from what C/C++ fixes or from an `unsafe` statement, the walk goes through every part, a caller that cannot vouch reaches only safe code, and vouching never overrides a known-false part. It costs nothing at run time except the NULL compare, and ordinary programs with no foreign code are unchanged.

There are 12 medium holes and 8 low ones; none is high. They cluster in four places.

1. The [FFI-57] handle model is not general:
   - owned handles cannot go into fields or spans, or else stale copies escape;
   - a handle's null value is not stated;
   - `destroys(f)` silently vouches `f`'s `@safety` at every end, and the XVI.4 rewrite itself gives a false `@safety` for vkDestroyBuffer and keeps vkCmdSetViewport safe;
   - `owns H` carries the device per value, against FFI-36a's one word and verdict 107.
2. J8-11's start-up-code half:
   - it depends on the toolchain (`<iostream>` before and after GCC 13);
   - it is inconsistent with linked libraries;
   - under the rule's own floor it vouches no memory-safety fact.
   I recommend keeping only the process-wide-name half. Separately, the wording catches template instantiations and Ember's own shim and thunk names.
3. Routes point 4 misses:
   - frames of an `unsafe gen fn` iterated by safe `for` (verdict 119's lambda rule refuses the same program);
   - `unsafe override` of a C++ virtual that C++ dispatch calls.
4. Spec-text and verdict consistency:
   - [LT-1] is not in the change list, so it still decides C++ members by rule 1;
   - `[[clang::lifetimebound]]` is a third fact source that UNS-13 does not list, and it clashes with verdict 132;
   - the `mutable` member is judged as a Cell, which refuses the idiomatic thread-safe C++ class (P01 overlap);
   - foreign types' thread facts get a second spelling (a wrapper struct) beside the overlay's existing `threads(...)` type words;
   - export contract words repeat what the export's own type says (checklist 5).

The design already discloses its verdict 137 narrowing and open risks 4, 6, 7 and 9. Where my holes touch those, I give the concrete program and a general fix rather than a new open risk. The GCC 13 libstdc++ change (stream initialisation moved into the library, PR 44952) is from memory and should be confirmed before it is quoted to the owner.

design.rule:
Safe code may rely on a fact about foreign code, a value or a type only in two cases. Either the compiler establishes it, from Ember code it reads or from what a C or C++ declaration's own text fixes under that language's rules (types and layouts, `const` on an object, `_Atomic`, `noexcept`, a C++ reference naming one object and never being null). Or someone states it in an `unsafe` item or block that has a `# SAFETY:` note, and `ember tcb` lists the statement. A statement means only what it says, and silence never picks a convenient reading. The compiler follows each fact through every part of a value, at any depth, and along every route a value takes between Ember and foreign code. So a value arriving from C has the type of everything C can send unless a statement narrows it, and foreign memory that foreign code reaches by itself may change at any foreign call. A safe use that needs an unknown fact is refused when compiling; the message names the fact and gives the line that states it. No statement overrides what the compiler knows to be false. A safe function is sound for every call that code outside its module can make, so a function whose soundness rests on its callers is an `unsafe fn` with `@safety`. Callers that write no Ember call (a value's end, a `dyn` or `virtual` call, an operator, a loop, a generator's `next`) reach only safe implementations of safe methods. Foreign code's calls are vouched where the function is handed to it: an export's note, the unsafe code that gives C a callback, a virtual's overlay entry. Each fact has one way to be stated, and it reaches every kind of item and every type:
- `unsafe extend T implements Send:` and `unsafe extend T implements ThreadShared:`, for any struct, enum or class, and in its overlay for a foreign type;
- contract words on every slot whose Ember type maps a given C type, and an export's own written signature, from which its C prototype is made;
- `immutable` for foreign memory that never changes, on a static or after `from(static)`;
- `unsafe import`, exactly when an import's compiled code defines a name the whole process shares.

