The ordinary program gets shorter: the extra lock and its helper function go, because the binding states once what it knows about its C object. A program that touches no foreign code reads exactly as before, and the rule adds no word to it. An export now writes no contract words: its types say it. The words the rule asks for fall only on the code that vouches:
- `from(this) exclusive` in a C++ overlay entry that returns a reference;
- `@safety` on an export whose body relies on more than its types say;
- `unsafe import c` on a header that defines a name the whole process shares;
- `@ffi(handle, destroys(f))` on a handle type that has owners;
- `immutable` after `from(static)` for memory that never changes.
A class that wraps a C handle writes its `drop` as the spec's examples already do: `fn drop():` with an `unsafe:` block inside.

design.misuse:
Everything the rule targets is refused when compiling, at its line, with the line to write:
- `safe fn` taking a struct that holds a pointer, or filling one with a view or handle field (E5002, naming the field);
- a C++ reference result without a lifetime word (E5002);
- `unsafe fn drop`, an `unsafe gen fn`, an unsafe override or implementation of a safe method, or an `unsafe` override of a C++ virtual whose entry states no `@safety`;
- a safe read of a C++ `const` global with a `mutable` member (E3100, naming the member);
- an import that defines a process-wide name, written without `unsafe` (E3100, naming it);
- `unsafe extend` over a `Cell`, `shares` or `sharedref mut` part (E2040);
- pointer words on a handle (E5002); a handle made from a number outside unsafe code (E3100); a buffer that outlives its device, or a copy of a handle used after its owner ends (E3060);
- a view of a "valid until the next call" result, or of a C++-held object's vector, used across another foreign call (E3021, naming the call);
- a function pointer from a plugin used after the plugin is unloaded (E3060);
- a safe call of a handle type's destructor (E3100).
A forgotten word never becomes a crash. The type stays the widest one (a raw pointer, an `unsafe extern "C" fn`, `Option[H]`, a read-only view, a view that ends at the next foreign call), or the declaration is refused, which is verdict 31's direction.

The rest is the trusted computing base: a `# SAFETY:` note, `@safety`, contract word or export type that is false. Examples are an export typed `msg: CStrView` whose host passes NULL, a `destroys(f)` whose `f` asks more than an owner's existence gives, and a safe function whose note rests on its callers (the warning catches it when the reliance is a raw-pointer or `unsafe` callable parameter). `ember tcb` lists each one line by line, as in Rust. The rule's job is that every such statement is written, visible and listed, and that the compiler refuses any statement it can see is false.

