safety.notes:
The core holds for every case the design lists. Written ends are checked as calls where they run. A drop that waits or calls C runs where its last owner ends, as with shared_ptr and Arc. Placed drops wait for nothing and call no C. The rest takeover never touches a home count of a running thread. Verdicts 43, 56, 57, 114 and 164-166 hold. Both changes to verdict 101 are declared for the owner. The round-0 high holes are closed: the lock across a yield no longer matters to queued drops, the drop runs after deregistering, and C is never called by a placed drop.

Two high holes remain:
1. Points 2 and 6 move the end of a thread_shared value, and of the one-thread objects it owns, off its maker. A plain weakref link and a count-free view on the maker then race with, or outlive, that end. Today the queue keeps such an end on the maker. It closes cleanly if P01 refuses a weakref to a one-thread part of a thread_shared value, as verdict 166 does for tasks.
2. The hand-over at making and the clear points depend on facts of the whole program. Code of a package compiled alone cannot have them, and I.6 rules out a correctness property that depends on whole-program optimisation. The fix is a TypeInfo bit the program's build writes.

The medium holes are all precision in the new protocol or at its seams:
- the takeover's merge when owners remain;
- RT-8's rest entry from empty against resting with a list;
- the wait to leave rest (priority inversion, possibly for ever);
- a contract export's return;
- ends the runtime performs;
- reload facts;
- P02's managers.

Each fix keeps the program text unchanged.

Not reported, for other groups:
- whether a free is Alloc under @realtime (P14);
- whether a bounded send and other waits are Block by property rather than by a list (P14);
- whether timing-dependent placed drops make a @deterministic result Nondet (P14, J6-07). The round-0 breaker found nothing beyond lock-guarded reads, and I agree.
- the lock-across-yield gap (THR-19).

One side note: J7-02's Texture prints in its drop. If std's println carries FFI or Block, a Texture is handed over at making, and the answer's 'neither waits nor calls C' line is wrong. The printed outcome is the same.

Process: I only read files and searched them, and ran no compiler, build or script. The Bash calls were read-only (sed, grep, wc, ls). One of them ran `python -c "print(1)"` while checking for jq; it read and changed nothing.

