design.rule:
Whatever crosses between Ember and foreign code has exactly one owner at every moment, written in Ember's own words. Every type has one end. Every owner of it runs that end, and the end decides where its owners come from:
- an Ember type is made and ended by Ember (its drop and Ember's free);
- a foreign type is made by foreign code and ended by the `destroys(f)` its declaration names;
- a C++ class is ended by its overlay's `destroys(f)`, else by `delete`, and every object of it that Ember makes comes from `new`.
Every hold that foreign code keeps has a release Ember can reach. What it keeps lives, at the address foreign code was given, until that release.

(1) Foreign code takes it for good (`owns`) only when it ends the value by the type's own end: a foreign type's value, or an object of an Ember class derived from C++, whose `delete` is its Ember end. Anything else is refused when compiling. Every foreign call counts as possibly ending such objects.

(2) Foreign code holds it while Ember owns it. The contract names the release:
- `destroys(f)`: when `f` returns;
- `callback(once)`: at its one run;
- `until(x)`: at a once callback's run, at foreign code's call of a function the compiler supplies, or at a foreign owner's end;
- `slot(a)`: at the next registration on `a`.
With `destroys(f)` the call returns a token, `Retained[T]`, the one Ember owner of the hold. Its end calls `f` once, unless the release has already come, and the token stays on the thread that made it. What C keeps is handed over at the call. It may have other Ember owners only when foreign code holds the address of a block that never moves. Then every foreign call counts as changing the part of it that foreign code reaches by itself. What `f` needs from the program is tied to the token: the token ends first.

(3) Ember takes it from foreign code. It becomes one owner, ended by its type's end. `adopt` takes an Ember type back only from what `into_raw()` gave (`OwnPtr[T]`), and a foreign type from the address foreign code made.

