design.misuse:
**Refused when compiling.**
- `sock=old.sock` with a read-only `old`: E3013, "`old` only reads the old session, so `old.sock` cannot be handed over; take the session over: `static fn migrate_from(owns old: OldSession) -> Result[Session, ReloadError]:`".
- Changing what a taken part brings with it (`items = old.items` then `items.push(owns Item())`, or `old.socks.pop()`): refused with a code of its own, "`items` holds `old.items`, whose elements the running program still reads until the reload is applied; carry it as it is (`items=old.items`) and add to it after the reload".
- A `migrate_from` that changes a static or an object from before the reload (`LOG.push(...)`): refused the same way, naming the line and what it changes.
- A `migrate_from` that panics: E2225, as today.

**Refused in plan.** The old image keeps running, and the report names the holder and the part:
- a default of an added field that changes what the program holds;
- a kept view or `sharedref mut` link into what reclaim would end, or into a part placed by `migrate_from`;
- a raw pointer to a changed value the walk did not reach through an owner;
- an object C keeps that must grow (HR-20);
- a lambda with live values that gained a capture;
- a reclaim drop that clashes with a frame below a checkpoint (E9030).

**Discarded in prepare.** Nothing changes, and the old image keeps running:
- `migrate_from` returns `Err`;
- a run-time check inside it fails (HR-35);
- an owner it makes reaches its count's limit ([HR-50]);
- the transaction arena runs out.

**After success.** A panic in a leftover's `drop` during reclaim aborts, as any panic in a `drop` does (HR-41, PAN-1).

No outcome leaves an old layout under new code, ends a value twice, leaks a count, or closes a socket the running program still uses.

