design.rule:
One rule: a promise that a value does not change holds against every route the program could change it by, for exactly as long as someone the promise was made to could see the change.

(1) Every route counts. The routes are: the name itself; anything made from the name (a view, an owner, a `weakref` or `sharedref mut` link, a capture), which may do no more than the name may; another name of the same place; a link to the value made anywhere, before or after the promise began; and a call that does any of these.

(2) The compiler finds the routes by following each object and each link from the line that makes it, as it already follows a view's source and a stored function. Nothing is written for this, and nothing runs while the program runs.

(3) Each promise binds for its own span:
- a read-only view, while it is used (a kept view, for its holder's life);
- a name that only reads, for the life of everything made from it;
- a `let` field, from the end of `init` until its object ends;
- a `static` or `const`, for the whole program;
- a walk, while the loop or generator is used;
- a `@cached` input, while a result is kept. A change through the holder clears the kept result, as today.

Once an object has ended, no name but its own ending code can see it, so `drop` may change its `let` fields as it may any field.

For `this`, the right is worked out from the method's body. A method that stores `weakref this` where the program later changes the object through it is a method that may change its object.

