design.rule:
The rule keeps two things apart.

(1) A right limits only its own route.
- A plain name changes nothing it views, through itself.
- Every route to a value is limited by the promises on its way. A route is a path through fields, elements and links, or a link, owner, view or capture made from a name. The promises are:
  - a parameter's word, which covers everything the function reaches through the parameter, links included, for every type (verdict 32);
  - for `this`, the route its call's object came by;
  - a `shares` owner;
  - a `let` field after `init`;
  - a `static` or a `const`;
  - each link, by the right it was made with.
- A plain local, loop, pattern or opened name makes no promise to anyone. A link made from it, or a route through a link inside what it views, keeps the right of the route it views.
- The owner, and every other route, keep what they may (verdict 23).

(2) A no-change promise protects a value against every route that may reach it, for its span. "May reach" is [BCK-3]'s existing rule: one type, at least one path through a link or a kept view. The promises are:
- a read-only view, while it is used;
- a walk;
- a view kept `from` a `let` field, for its holder's life;
- a `@cached` method's inputs, while a result is kept.

`let`, `static` and `const` need only (1), because every route to what they hold starts at them. A `let` field's promise ends when its object ends, so `drop` may change it.

Nothing runs while the program runs.

