design.rule:
An effect is what an operation does. Each effect (Alloc, Panic(Explicit), RuntimeCheck(k), Nondet, and the rest of the X.1 table) is defined once, by a property: it takes memory from the heap; it stops because the program's own words ask it to; it tests a safety property while running; its result is not fixed by the program's input. That definition alone decides which operations carry the effect. A name never decides it, nor does whether some list mentions the operation, nor a choice a compiler makes. Every fact a definition turns on is fixed by the language and worked out by the compiler from the code:
- where a function value's state lives: its state word, its writer's frame, or a block;
- whether IEEE 754 fixes a floating-point result's bits;
- which orders between two threads the program fixes: a start, a join, a blocking receive, a reduction's combine order.

Effects pass to everything that runs the operation (EFF-1, EFF-2). A contract or a library layer forbids exactly its effects: every operation that does what they define, and nothing else. So a contract means all of what it says, and only that. No program gains a word.

