owner_short.today:
A class makes every field default before `init` runs, even one that `init` immediately replaces.

Four things are not decided:
* what a struct's `init` does with a default;
* what `super.init` calls when the base wrote no `init`;
* whether a `pub(package)` field's constructor works inside the package;
* what a `@ser(skip)` field holds.

Also, `@derive(Default)` and `Deserialize` build a value without running the type's `init`, so loading `{"level": 5000}` gives a `Volume` that its own `init` would have refused.

owner_short.recommendation:
A. It is C++'s initialiser-list behaviour without the list, plus Kotlin's 'deserialising runs the constructor'. It costs nothing over C and adds no word. Its visible changes:
* a default with a side effect (`id: int = next_id()`) no longer runs when `init` sets the field first;
* `@derive(Default)` and `Deserialize` run your `init`;
* `this.init()` is refused;
* a `pub open` class sets its fields before calling a method a subclass may replace.

repairs.answer:
[CON-5]: a call in init that may run a method body on `this` needs every field the bodies use. For a method another package may override (some `pub` `open` or `abstract` class has it), those bodies cannot be read when the base is compiled, so the call needs every field set: E2100 at `Widget.init`, "move line 6 below line 7". Inside a package, the overrides' bodies decide, as CLS-2 and DSP-5 do today. CLS-11's sentence is reworded to 'every field it uses holds a value', which is now true.

This is the breaker's stricter alternative, chosen over recording unset fields in the interface file:
* the cost falls on the rare, dangerous base, not on the ordinary dependant override that reads a base field;
* the error lands where it can be fixed, with a line that compiles (checklist 9);
* a `pub` promise not resting on code the compiler cannot read is the language's existing edge rule (MOD-9, IFC-6, verdicts 108 and 113);
* Swift's two-phase init has the same rule.

The record-and-check design is owner option E.

