design.cost:
**Run time: zero, for every case the rule decides.**
- A compile-time result is constant data in the image. CRC is 1 KB of read-only data (256 entries of 4 bytes), the same bytes as C's precomputed `static const uint32_t crc_table[256]`, read with the same load.
- `sky_shader()` returns a 16-byte view (address and length) of text placed once in read-only data. That is what C23's #embed into a `static const char[]` gives: no file read, no allocation, no Io.
- The CT-8 checks belong to the evaluator and leave nothing in the program. The compiled unsafe loop is the same machine code as C's.

**The rule also removes run-time work that today's gate forces.** Today, a static is built at first access, not at compile time, when its initialiser reaches any of these:
- a printing branch that is never taken;
- a library's private unsafe block;
- a stored callback list;
- a @parallel loop.

Built at first access, it costs the build at start-up plus STA-3's once-check (one load and one branch) on every access. Under the rule, such a static is constant data.

**Run-time memory:** the same bytes as C, with one copy per file however many places read it.

**Compile time (the only cost):**
- per pointer access in the evaluator: a lookup of the block the pointer came from, and compares for bounds, alignment and liveness;
- per read: a test of the initialised-bytes map (one bit per byte of evaluator memory);
- per view that unsafe code makes: a tag on the place it views;
- at the end: one walk for blocks that were neither ended nor kept.

This is not measured here. It is bounded by CT-3's limits per evaluation: 10^8 steps and 256 MB.

C pays nothing at compile time because it computes nothing there. C programs build such tables with a generator script or at start-up.

