other_cases.settled:
The loop runs: compares and differences within one block, one past its end included, are answered from the block (CT-2). These observe addresses and are E6010 where reached:
- `p < q` across two blocks;
- == of one past a block's end with another block;
- reading a pointer's bytes at another type.
A value that holds a pointer's bytes at a type that holds no pointer is also caught by the placement walk.

Each is checked (CT-8). These are E6011 at the line:
- an index out of range;
- overlapping views;
- a bool that is neither 0 nor 1;
- bytes never written, read at a type.
The padded copy compiles: an untyped copy carries each byte as it is, padding included, and validity is checked where a value is read at its type. The compiled program keeps these operations unchecked, as written (UNS-2).

design.cost:
**Run time: zero for every case the rule decides, and less than today in several.**
- A compile-time result is constant data in the image. CRC is 1 KB of read-only data (256 entries of 4 bytes), the same bytes as C's precomputed `static const uint32_t crc_table[256]`, read with the same load.
- `sky_shader()` returns a 16-byte view (address and length) of text placed once in read-only data. That is what C23's #embed into a `static const char[]` gives: no file read, no allocation, no Io.
- A static whose state changes in place (an Atomic, a lock, a thread_shared object, a function value holding one) is placed in writable data with no initialisation code and no once-check. That is C's `static _Atomic int hits = 0;`, or C++20's constinit. Today's text builds such a static at first access, which costs the build at start-up plus STA-3's once-check (one load and one branch) on every access.
- A thread_shared value a static holds keeps its 32-byte header (verdict 101), placed handed over (RC-4 point 4). Each owner made of it is one atomic add. That is what a static built at first access and shared between threads pays today.
- A static is built at first access only where a block of it may grow or be freed, or where its value needs a run. It then costs what it costs today.
- The CT-8 and CT-10 checks belong to the evaluator and leave nothing in the program. The compiled unsafe loop is the same machine code as C's.
- UNS-4's restated pointer sentence is C's model for raw pointers, so the compiled code is C's. Safe code keeps its view rules.

**The rule also removes run-time work that today's gate forces.** Today a static is built at first access, not at compile time, when its initialiser reaches any of these:
- a printing branch that is never taken;
- a library's private unsafe block;
- a stored callback list;
- a @parallel loop;
- making a thread_shared value.
Under the rule, such a static is constant or writable data.

**Run-time memory:** the same bytes as C, with one copy per file however many places read it, and one description per type however many places reflect it.

**Compile time (the only cost):**
- per pointer access: a lookup of the block the pointer came from, and compares for bounds, alignment and liveness;
- per read at a type: a test of each byte's state;
- per view made, in safe or unsafe code: a tag on its place;
- in an evaluation that starts threads: per access and per atomic, lock or channel step, a compare of vector clocks, of one entry per thread;
- at the end: one walk of the value, for placement, dangling views, owners and leaks.

This is not measured here. It is bounded by CT-3's limits per evaluation: 10^8 steps and 256 MB. C pays nothing at compile time because it computes nothing there; C programs build such tables with a generator script or at start-up.

