repairs.answer:
Real. Rule 6 makes the holder an implementer, as Rust's `impl Iterator for Box<I>` does:
- `owns dyn I` implements `I`;
- a method with a table entry goes through the table, so each kind's own version runs;
- a method with a body and no single entry (every STD-19 adapter and consumer) runs the interface's body, compiled once for `Self = owns dyn I`, with no entry and no allocation.

IV.11 finds these on the holder before stripping `owns`. `[TYP-22]`'s generic bullet now covers every non-view use of a type parameter (taken over, stored, returned): such a method runs its body if it has one, and is `E2050` only if it has none.

In the programs, `g.take(3).sum()` prints 6 and 9, `it.map(owns fn(x) => x * 2).sum()` prints 180, and `[I: needs Iterator]` takes the holder. A kind's own version of a generic adapter is reached only through a known kind, as in Rust (open risk 5).

design.cost:
Run time: nothing beyond what the line already does. The conversion is the one the `T` place does:
- rule 8: the table pointer stored beside the data pointer;
- rule 10: the function value;
- rule 12: the number converted as `as` converts it, losing nothing;
- rules 6 and 7: a pointer and a length, or a pointer.

The wrap is exactly the written `Some(...)`. For a type with a niche (`owns T`, `owns dyn I`, a class object, `ref`, `Span`, `StrView`, [TYP-13]) it costs nothing, because `None` is the null pointer. For other types it is one tag byte stored (`Option[u64]`).

C pays the same: `slot->weapon = p; slot->weapon_vt = &sword_vt;` with NULL as none, or a `has_cap` flag beside a `uint64_t`.

Memory: zero. `Option[owns dyn Weapon]` is 16 bytes, as `owns dyn Weapon` is. No check runs while the program runs, and no allocation is added: a list literal allocates exactly as the plain literal does.

Compile time: one more step of the existing coercion check at a site whose type is an `Option`. There is no search and no backtracking.

One cost that is not this rule's: Ember function values are not in [TYP-13]'s niche list, so `Option[OnHit]` may carry a tag where C uses a null pointer. Writing `Some(log_hit)` by hand pays the same tag.

