Giving an existing `Option` place to a reading parameter or view costs a tag (or null) test and a select, to pass the payload's address or null: C's `o.has ? &o.val : NULL`, Rust's `.as_ref()`. The callee's later `exists as` is a null test, as C's `if (p)`. Passing a view of an `Option` on, or calling `as_ref()` on one, is free.

Memory: zero. `Option[owns dyn Weapon]` is 16 bytes, as `owns dyn Weapon` is. No check runs while the program runs, and no allocation is added.

Compile time: one more step of the existing coercion check at a site whose type is an `Option`, and one fixed three-step order for every conversion. There is no search and no backtracking.

