`for v in t:` and `for v in values:` are 'written out once per element', and there is no iterator whose end ends what the loop has not reached. The rule leaves four things undecided:
- what `return`, `break` or `?` part-way through a walk over owned elements does with the rest;
- whether `for ... else` (CTL-4) applies;
- whether `break` and `continue` are allowed;
- whether the walked tuple may change between rounds. CTL-2 speaks of collections; BRW-1 alone allows `t = other` after one round's last use, so the next round reads the new tuple, unlike Python's walk.
An implementation that ends the hidden whole at the exit ends the jobs already reached a second time; one that ends nothing leaks.

